1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,710

2
00:00:05,710 --> 00:00:08,109
So today, we're going to give
an instructor-led lecture,

3
00:00:08,109 --> 00:00:12,550
talking about some key topics
in Transformers and LLMs

4
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:13,720
these days.

5
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:15,820
In particular, we'll be
talking about agents,

6
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:17,830
and I'll be discussing
Emergent Abilities,

7
00:00:17,830 --> 00:00:21,640
Intermediate-Guided
reasoning, as well as BabyLM.

8
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:27,745
So let me actually go to my part
because Div is not here yet.

9
00:00:27,745 --> 00:00:31,970

10
00:00:31,970 --> 00:00:33,740
So I'm sure many
of you have read

11
00:00:33,740 --> 00:00:37,310
this paper, Emergent Abilities
of Large Language Models,

12
00:00:37,310 --> 00:00:38,420
from 2022.

13
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:41,930
So I'll briefly go
through some of them.

14
00:00:41,930 --> 00:00:44,570
So basically, an
ability is emergent

15
00:00:44,570 --> 00:00:47,600
if it is present and larger,
but not smaller models.

16
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:49,490
And it would not have
been directly predicted

17
00:00:49,490 --> 00:00:53,060
by extrapolating performance
from smaller models.

18
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:54,710
So you can think of performance.

19
00:00:54,710 --> 00:00:57,050
It's basically near-random
until a certain threshold,

20
00:00:57,050 --> 00:00:58,580
called a critical threshold.

21
00:00:58,580 --> 00:01:00,650
And then it improves
very heavily.

22
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:02,630
This is known as a
phase transition.

23
00:01:02,630 --> 00:01:05,180
And again, it would not have
been extrapolated or predicted

24
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:08,750
if you were to extend the curve
of the performance of smaller

25
00:01:08,750 --> 00:01:09,470
models.

26
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:12,590
It's more of a jump,
which we'll see later.

27
00:01:12,590 --> 00:01:14,810
So here's an example
of few-shot prompting

28
00:01:14,810 --> 00:01:16,190
for many different tasks.

29
00:01:16,190 --> 00:01:19,820
For example, modular
arithmetic, unscrambling words,

30
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:22,490
different tasks and so forth.

31
00:01:22,490 --> 00:01:26,210
And you'll see that
performance jumps very heavily

32
00:01:26,210 --> 00:01:28,070
up until a certain point.

33
00:01:28,070 --> 00:01:30,170
I believe the x-axis
here is the number

34
00:01:30,170 --> 00:01:33,080
of training flops, which
correspond to basically model

35
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:34,280
scale.

36
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:37,850
So you'll see in many cases
around 10 to the 22 or 10

37
00:01:37,850 --> 00:01:39,230
to the 23 training flops.

38
00:01:39,230 --> 00:01:44,390
There's a massive
exponential jump or increase

39
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:48,230
in terms of model performance
on these tasks, which was not

40
00:01:48,230 --> 00:01:51,290
present on smaller scales.

41
00:01:51,290 --> 00:01:54,080
So it's quite unpredictable.

42
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:57,080
And here are some examples
of this occurring using

43
00:01:57,080 --> 00:01:58,760
augmented prompting strategies.

44
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:01,790
So I'll be talking a bit
later about chain of thought.

45
00:02:01,790 --> 00:02:04,310
But basically, these
strategies improve

46
00:02:04,310 --> 00:02:09,979
the ability of getting behavior
from models on different tasks.

47
00:02:09,979 --> 00:02:13,190
So you see, for example, with
Chain-of-Thought Reasoning,

48
00:02:13,190 --> 00:02:15,950
that's an emergent behavior
that happens again around 10

49
00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:17,510
to the 22 training flops.

50
00:02:17,510 --> 00:02:22,250
And without it, model
performance on GSM8K,

51
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:24,410
which is a
mathematics benchmark,

52
00:02:24,410 --> 00:02:28,100
it doesn't really improve
heavily, but chain of thought

53
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:31,790
leads to that emergent
behavior or sudden increase

54
00:02:31,790 --> 00:02:33,090
in performance.

55
00:02:33,090 --> 00:02:35,670
And here's just a table
from the paper, which

56
00:02:35,670 --> 00:02:39,780
has a bigger list of
emergent abilities of LLMs,

57
00:02:39,780 --> 00:02:42,030
as well as their scale
at which they occur.

58
00:02:42,030 --> 00:02:43,710
So I recommend
that you check out

59
00:02:43,710 --> 00:02:45,930
the paper to learn a bit more.

60
00:02:45,930 --> 00:02:47,910
And so one thing
researchers have

61
00:02:47,910 --> 00:02:51,360
been wondering is, why does
this emergence occur exactly?

62
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:53,520
And even now, there's
few explanations

63
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:55,360
for why that happens.

64
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:58,260
And the authors also found that
the evaluation metrics used

65
00:02:58,260 --> 00:03:00,000
to measure these
abilities may not fully

66
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:01,530
explain why they emerge.

67
00:03:01,530 --> 00:03:04,740
And they suggest some
alternative evaluation metrics,

68
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:08,160
which I encourage you to
read more in the paper.

69
00:03:08,160 --> 00:03:11,700
So other than scaling
up, to encourage

70
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:14,550
these emergent abilities,
which could endow

71
00:03:14,550 --> 00:03:17,880
even larger LMs with further
new emergent abilities,

72
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:20,070
what else can be done?

73
00:03:20,070 --> 00:03:23,160
Well, things like investigating
new architectures,

74
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:26,700
higher quality data, which is
very important for performance

75
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:29,700
on all tasks, improved
training and improved training

76
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:32,250
procedures, could enable
emergent abilities

77
00:03:32,250 --> 00:03:36,000
to occur, especially on
smaller models, which

78
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,570
is a current growing area
of research, which I'll also

79
00:03:39,570 --> 00:03:41,880
talk about a bit more later.

80
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:43,830
Other abilities
include potentially

81
00:03:43,830 --> 00:03:46,140
improving the few-shot
prompting abilities

82
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:49,800
of LMs, theoretical and
interpretability research

83
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:54,690
again, to try to understand why
emergent abilities is a thing

84
00:03:54,690 --> 00:03:58,050
and how we can maybe
leverage that further,

85
00:03:58,050 --> 00:04:01,850
as well as maybe some
computational linguistics work.

86
00:04:01,850 --> 00:04:05,000
So with these large models
and emergent abilities,

87
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:06,800
there's also risks.

88
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:09,230
There's potential
societal risks.

89
00:04:09,230 --> 00:04:13,580
For example, truthfulness,
bias, and toxicity risks,

90
00:04:13,580 --> 00:04:17,029
as emergent abilities
incentivizes further scaling

91
00:04:17,029 --> 00:04:21,320
up language models, for example,
up to GPT-4 size or further.

92
00:04:21,320 --> 00:04:25,220
However, this may lead to
bias increasing, as well as

93
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:28,940
toxicity and the memorization
of training data.

94
00:04:28,940 --> 00:04:31,460
That's one thing that
these larger models are

95
00:04:31,460 --> 00:04:35,450
more potent at, and
there's potential risks

96
00:04:35,450 --> 00:04:37,160
in future language
models that have

97
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:38,510
also not been discovered yet.

98
00:04:38,510 --> 00:04:43,610
So it's important that we
approach this in a safe manner

99
00:04:43,610 --> 00:04:45,240
as well.

100
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:47,900
And, of course, emergent
abilities and larger models

101
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:50,930
have also led to
sociological changes,

102
00:04:50,930 --> 00:04:54,500
changes in the community's
views, and use of these models.

103
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:56,810
Most importantly, it's
led to the development

104
00:04:56,810 --> 00:04:59,180
of general-purpose
models, which perform

105
00:04:59,180 --> 00:05:02,640
on a wide range of tasks,
not just particular tasks

106
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:03,570
it was trained for.

107
00:05:03,570 --> 00:05:06,570
For example, when you
think of ChatGPT, GPT-3.5,

108
00:05:06,570 --> 00:05:09,540
as well as GPT-4, they are
more general-purpose models,

109
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:11,640
which work well
across the board,

110
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:15,540
and can then be further
adapted to different use cases,

111
00:05:15,540 --> 00:05:20,280
mainly through in-context
prompting and so forth.

112
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:23,370
This has also led to new
applications of language models

113
00:05:23,370 --> 00:05:24,570
outside of NLP.

114
00:05:24,570 --> 00:05:26,310
For example, they're
being used a lot now

115
00:05:26,310 --> 00:05:28,590
for text-to-image generation.

116
00:05:28,590 --> 00:05:32,070
The encoder part of those
text-to-image models

117
00:05:32,070 --> 00:05:35,940
are basically transformer
models or large language models,

118
00:05:35,940 --> 00:05:39,000
as well as things like
robotics and so forth.

119
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:40,410
So you'll know that--

120
00:05:40,410 --> 00:05:42,720
earlier this quarter,
Jim Phang gave

121
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:47,100
a talk about how they're using
GPT-4 and so forth in Minecraft

122
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:51,360
and for robotics work, as well
as long-range horizon tasks

123
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:53,050
for robotics.

124
00:05:53,050 --> 00:05:53,550
And yeah.

125
00:05:53,550 --> 00:05:55,050
So basically, in
general, it's led

126
00:05:55,050 --> 00:05:58,740
to a shift in the NLP community
towards general purpose rather

127
00:05:58,740 --> 00:06:02,110
than task-specific models.

128
00:06:02,110 --> 00:06:05,710
And as of stated earlier, some
directions for future work

129
00:06:05,710 --> 00:06:09,010
include model scaling,
further model scaling.

130
00:06:09,010 --> 00:06:13,900
Although, I believe that we
will soon probably be reaching

131
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:16,930
a limit or point of diminishing
returns with just more model

132
00:06:16,930 --> 00:06:19,870
scale, improved model
architectures and training

133
00:06:19,870 --> 00:06:22,600
methods, data scaling.

134
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:27,040
So I also believe that data
quality is of high importance,

135
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:30,430
possibly even more
important than the model

136
00:06:30,430 --> 00:06:33,940
scale and the model itself,
better techniques for

137
00:06:33,940 --> 00:06:37,000
and understanding of
prompting, as well as exploring

138
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,430
and enabling performance
on frontier tasks

139
00:06:39,430 --> 00:06:42,220
that current models are not
able to perform well on.

140
00:06:42,220 --> 00:06:45,220
So GPT-4 pushed
the limit of this.

141
00:06:45,220 --> 00:06:47,530
It's able to perform
well on many more tasks,

142
00:06:47,530 --> 00:06:50,500
but studies have
shown that it still

143
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:55,000
suffers from even some more
basic reasoning, analogical,

144
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,030
and common-sense reasoning.

145
00:06:58,030 --> 00:07:00,580
So I just had some
questions here.

146
00:07:00,580 --> 00:07:03,970
I'm not sure how much
time we have to address.

147
00:07:03,970 --> 00:07:06,850
So for the first
one, like I said,

148
00:07:06,850 --> 00:07:09,340
emergent abilities, I think,
will arise to a certain point,

149
00:07:09,340 --> 00:07:13,990
but there will be a limit or
point of diminishing returns

150
00:07:13,990 --> 00:07:16,330
as model scale, as
well as data scale

151
00:07:16,330 --> 00:07:18,460
rises, because I
believe at some point,

152
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:19,750
there will be overfitting.

153
00:07:19,750 --> 00:07:21,550
And there's only so
much you can learn

154
00:07:21,550 --> 00:07:24,550
from all the data on the web.

155
00:07:24,550 --> 00:07:27,880
So I believe that more
creative approaches

156
00:07:27,880 --> 00:07:32,710
will be necessary after a
certain point, which also

157
00:07:32,710 --> 00:07:34,000
addresses the second question.

158
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,550

159
00:07:36,550 --> 00:07:37,615
So I will move on.

160
00:07:37,615 --> 00:07:41,150

161
00:07:41,150 --> 00:07:42,800
If anybody has any
questions, also,

162
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:46,392
feel free to
interrupt at any time.

163
00:07:46,392 --> 00:07:48,100
So the second thing
I'll be talking about

164
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:50,230
is this thing called
Intermediate-Guided Reasoning.

165
00:07:50,230 --> 00:07:52,780
So I don't think this
is actually a term.

166
00:07:52,780 --> 00:07:55,870
It's typically called
Chain-of-Thought Reasoning,

167
00:07:55,870 --> 00:07:57,790
but it's not just--

168
00:07:57,790 --> 00:07:58,960
chains are now being used.

169
00:07:58,960 --> 00:08:02,020
So I wanted to give
it a more broad title.

170
00:08:02,020 --> 00:08:04,990
So I called it
Intermediate-Guided Reasoning.

171
00:08:04,990 --> 00:08:07,240
So this was inspired
by this work

172
00:08:07,240 --> 00:08:09,520
also by my friend, Jason,
who was at Google now

173
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:12,220
at OpenAI, called
Chain-of-Thought Reasoning

174
00:08:12,220 --> 00:08:13,780
or CoT.

175
00:08:13,780 --> 00:08:17,320
This is basically a series of
intermediate reasoning steps,

176
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:19,960
which has been shown to
improve LLM performance,

177
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:23,103
especially on more
complex reasoning tasks.

178
00:08:23,103 --> 00:08:24,520
It's inspired by
the human thought

179
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:27,520
process, which is to
decompose many problems

180
00:08:27,520 --> 00:08:29,830
into multi-step problems.

181
00:08:29,830 --> 00:08:32,650
For example, when you
answer an exam, when

182
00:08:32,650 --> 00:08:34,570
you're solving math
questions on an exam,

183
00:08:34,570 --> 00:08:36,400
you don't just go
to the final answer.

184
00:08:36,400 --> 00:08:38,289
You write out your steps.

185
00:08:38,289 --> 00:08:40,390
Even when you're just
thinking through things,

186
00:08:40,390 --> 00:08:43,480
you break it down into a
piece-wise or step-by-step

187
00:08:43,480 --> 00:08:46,580
fashion, which allows
you to typically arrive

188
00:08:46,580 --> 00:08:51,080
at a more accurate final
answer and more easily arrive

189
00:08:51,080 --> 00:08:53,630
at the final answer
in the first place.

190
00:08:53,630 --> 00:08:56,540
Another advantage is this
provides an interpretable window

191
00:08:56,540 --> 00:08:58,430
into the behavior of the model.

192
00:08:58,430 --> 00:09:00,980
You can see exactly how
it arrived at an answer.

193
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:03,230
And if it did so
incorrectly, where

194
00:09:03,230 --> 00:09:08,310
in its reasoning path that
it goes wrong or starts

195
00:09:08,310 --> 00:09:11,190
going down an incorrect path
of reasoning, basically?

196
00:09:11,190 --> 00:09:13,117
And it basically
exploits the fact

197
00:09:13,117 --> 00:09:14,700
that deep down in
the model's weights,

198
00:09:14,700 --> 00:09:17,460
it knows more about
the problem than simply

199
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:19,620
prompting it to get a response.

200
00:09:19,620 --> 00:09:21,750
So here's an example
where on the left side,

201
00:09:21,750 --> 00:09:23,790
you can see the
standard prompting.

202
00:09:23,790 --> 00:09:27,240
You ask it a math question,
and it just simply

203
00:09:27,240 --> 00:09:28,440
gives you an answer.

204
00:09:28,440 --> 00:09:30,120
Whereas on the
right, you actually

205
00:09:30,120 --> 00:09:31,530
break it down step by step.

206
00:09:31,530 --> 00:09:35,250
You get it to show it steps
to solve the mathematical word

207
00:09:35,250 --> 00:09:37,440
problem step by step.

208
00:09:37,440 --> 00:09:41,070
And you'll see here that it
actually gets the right answer

209
00:09:41,070 --> 00:09:44,680
unlike standard prompting.

210
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:47,110
So there's many different ways
we can potentially improve

211
00:09:47,110 --> 00:09:48,940
Chain-of-Thought Reasoning.

212
00:09:48,940 --> 00:09:53,170
In particular, it's also
an emergent behavior

213
00:09:53,170 --> 00:09:55,870
that results in performance
gains for larger language

214
00:09:55,870 --> 00:09:56,800
models.

215
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:59,350
But still, even
in larger models,

216
00:09:59,350 --> 00:10:02,380
there's still a non-negligible
fraction of errors.

217
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:05,440
These come from calculator
errors, symbol mapping errors,

218
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:08,080
one missing step
errors, as well as

219
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:11,290
bigger errors due to larger
semantic understanding

220
00:10:11,290 --> 00:10:14,530
issues and generally
incoherent chains of thought.

221
00:10:14,530 --> 00:10:16,210
And we can potentially
investigate

222
00:10:16,210 --> 00:10:18,880
methods to address these.

223
00:10:18,880 --> 00:10:20,980
So as I said,
Chain-of-Thought mainly

224
00:10:20,980 --> 00:10:23,710
works for huge models
of approximately 100

225
00:10:23,710 --> 00:10:26,680
billion parameters or more.

226
00:10:26,680 --> 00:10:28,390
And there's three
potential reasons

227
00:10:28,390 --> 00:10:31,000
they do not work very
well for smaller models.

228
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,070
And that smaller models
are fundamentally

229
00:10:33,070 --> 00:10:35,530
more limited and incapable.

230
00:10:35,530 --> 00:10:38,800
They fail at even relatively
easier symbol mapping tasks,

231
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:40,870
as well as arithmetic tasks.

232
00:10:40,870 --> 00:10:44,410
They inherently are able to
do math less effectively.

233
00:10:44,410 --> 00:10:46,600
And they often have
logical loopholes

234
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:48,730
and just never arrive
at a final answer.

235
00:10:48,730 --> 00:10:51,280
For example, it goes on and on.

236
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:53,260
It's like an infinite
loop of logic that never

237
00:10:53,260 --> 00:10:55,930
actually converges anywhere.

238
00:10:55,930 --> 00:10:58,660
So if we're able to potentially
improve Chain-of-Thought

239
00:10:58,660 --> 00:11:01,930
for smaller models, this could
provide significant value

240
00:11:01,930 --> 00:11:04,900
to the research community.

241
00:11:04,900 --> 00:11:07,858
Another thing is to
potentially generalize it.

242
00:11:07,858 --> 00:11:10,150
Right now, Chain-of-Thought
has a more rigid definition

243
00:11:10,150 --> 00:11:10,660
and format.

244
00:11:10,660 --> 00:11:15,070
It's very step-by-step,
very concrete and defined.

245
00:11:15,070 --> 00:11:18,280
As a result, its advantages are
for particular domains and types

246
00:11:18,280 --> 00:11:19,180
of questions.

247
00:11:19,180 --> 00:11:23,020
For example, the task usually
must be challenging and require

248
00:11:23,020 --> 00:11:25,480
multi-step reasoning, and
it typically works better

249
00:11:25,480 --> 00:11:27,370
for things like
arithmetic, and not

250
00:11:27,370 --> 00:11:32,320
so much for things like response
generation, QA and so forth.

251
00:11:32,320 --> 00:11:36,430
And furthermore, it works
better for problems or tasks

252
00:11:36,430 --> 00:11:39,935
that have a relatively
flat scaling curve.

253
00:11:39,935 --> 00:11:41,310
Whereas when you
think of humans,

254
00:11:41,310 --> 00:11:44,430
we think through different
types of problems in multiple

255
00:11:44,430 --> 00:11:47,190
different ways, our quote,
unquote "scratchpad"

256
00:11:47,190 --> 00:11:49,710
that we use to think about
and arrive at a final answer

257
00:11:49,710 --> 00:11:50,820
for a problem.

258
00:11:50,820 --> 00:11:53,280
It's more flexible and
open to different reasoning

259
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:57,480
structures compared to such
a rigid step-by-step format.

260
00:11:57,480 --> 00:12:00,120
So hence, we can maybe
potentially generalize

261
00:12:00,120 --> 00:12:01,620
Chain-of-Thought
to be more flexible

262
00:12:01,620 --> 00:12:05,050
and work for more
types of problems.

263
00:12:05,050 --> 00:12:08,160
So now, I'll briefly discuss
some alternative or extension

264
00:12:08,160 --> 00:12:09,570
works to Chain-of-Thought.

265
00:12:09,570 --> 00:12:11,520
One is called Tree of Thought.

266
00:12:11,520 --> 00:12:13,230
This basically is
more like a tree,

267
00:12:13,230 --> 00:12:15,660
which considers multiple
different reasoning paths.

268
00:12:15,660 --> 00:12:19,290
It also has the ability to look
ahead and backtrack, and then

269
00:12:19,290 --> 00:12:23,700
go on other areas or other
branches of the tree,

270
00:12:23,700 --> 00:12:25,230
as necessary.

271
00:12:25,230 --> 00:12:27,360
So this leads to
more flexibility,

272
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:31,800
and it's shown to improve
performance on different tasks,

273
00:12:31,800 --> 00:12:34,230
including arithmetic tasks.

274
00:12:34,230 --> 00:12:37,470
There's also this work
by my friend called

275
00:12:37,470 --> 00:12:39,370
Socratic Questioning,
and it's sort

276
00:12:39,370 --> 00:12:43,060
of a divide-and-conquer
fashion algorithm simulating

277
00:12:43,060 --> 00:12:45,370
the recursive thinking
process of humans.

278
00:12:45,370 --> 00:12:47,620
So it uses a large-scale
language model

279
00:12:47,620 --> 00:12:51,650
to propose subproblems,
given a more complicated

280
00:12:51,650 --> 00:12:52,780
original problem.

281
00:12:52,780 --> 00:12:55,270
And just like Tree
of Thought, it also

282
00:12:55,270 --> 00:12:58,030
has recursive
backtracking and so forth.

283
00:12:58,030 --> 00:13:01,390
And the purpose is to
answer all the subproblems

284
00:13:01,390 --> 00:13:07,150
and go in an upwards fashion
to arrive at a final answer

285
00:13:07,150 --> 00:13:10,240
to the original problem.

286
00:13:10,240 --> 00:13:15,880
There's also this line of
work, which actually uses code,

287
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:19,210
as well as programs to help
arrive at a final answer.

288
00:13:19,210 --> 00:13:21,940
For example, Program-Aided
Language Models.

289
00:13:21,940 --> 00:13:23,770
It generates intermediate
reasoning steps

290
00:13:23,770 --> 00:13:26,650
in the form of code, which is
then offloaded to a runtime,

291
00:13:26,650 --> 00:13:29,020
such as a Python interpreter.

292
00:13:29,020 --> 00:13:31,720
And the point here is to
decompose the natural language

293
00:13:31,720 --> 00:13:33,460
problem into runnable steps.

294
00:13:33,460 --> 00:13:37,600
So hence, the amount of work
for the large language model

295
00:13:37,600 --> 00:13:38,890
is lower.

296
00:13:38,890 --> 00:13:41,140
Its purpose now
is simply to learn

297
00:13:41,140 --> 00:13:42,850
how to decompose
the natural language

298
00:13:42,850 --> 00:13:44,710
problem into those
runnable steps,

299
00:13:44,710 --> 00:13:48,260
and these steps themselves
are then fed to, for example,

300
00:13:48,260 --> 00:13:51,940
a Python interpreter
in order to solve them.

301
00:13:51,940 --> 00:13:54,640
And Program-of-Thought
here, PoT,

302
00:13:54,640 --> 00:14:00,190
is very similar to this, and
that it breaks it down into step

303
00:14:00,190 --> 00:14:02,620
by step of code instead
of natural language,

304
00:14:02,620 --> 00:14:07,510
which is then executed
by an actual code

305
00:14:07,510 --> 00:14:09,710
interpreter or program.

306
00:14:09,710 --> 00:14:17,070
So this, again, works
well for many tasks that--

307
00:14:17,070 --> 00:14:20,960
for example, things like
arithmetic, as you see that.

308
00:14:20,960 --> 00:14:24,200
Those are both of the examples
for both of these papers.

309
00:14:24,200 --> 00:14:26,870
And just like what
I said earlier,

310
00:14:26,870 --> 00:14:28,970
these also do not work
very well for things

311
00:14:28,970 --> 00:14:32,510
like response generation,
open-ended question answering

312
00:14:32,510 --> 00:14:35,090
and so forth.

313
00:14:35,090 --> 00:14:37,670
And there's other work, for
example, Faith and Fate.

314
00:14:37,670 --> 00:14:39,380
This actually
breaks down problems

315
00:14:39,380 --> 00:14:43,100
into substeps in the form
of computation graphs, which

316
00:14:43,100 --> 00:14:45,830
they show also works well
for things like arithmetic.

317
00:14:45,830 --> 00:14:47,540
So you see that
there's a trend here

318
00:14:47,540 --> 00:14:50,210
of this intermediate-guided
reasoning working

319
00:14:50,210 --> 00:14:53,810
very well for mathematical,
as well as logical problems,

320
00:14:53,810 --> 00:14:56,420
but not so much
for other things.

321
00:14:56,420 --> 00:14:59,780
So again, I encourage you
guys to maybe check out

322
00:14:59,780 --> 00:15:02,390
the original papers if
you want to learn more.

323
00:15:02,390 --> 00:15:06,020
There's a lot of interesting
work in this area these days.

324
00:15:06,020 --> 00:15:10,430
And I'll also be posting these
slides, as well as sending them.

325
00:15:10,430 --> 00:15:13,460
We'll probably post them on the
website, as well as Discord,

326
00:15:13,460 --> 00:15:17,760
but I'll also send them
through an email later.

327
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:20,160
So very lastly, I want to
touch upon this thing called

328
00:15:20,160 --> 00:15:24,270
the BabyLM Challenge
or Baby Language Model.

329
00:15:24,270 --> 00:15:26,910
So like I said earlier,
I think, at some point,

330
00:15:26,910 --> 00:15:29,970
scale will reach a point
of diminishing returns,

331
00:15:29,970 --> 00:15:32,250
as well as the fact
that further scale comes

332
00:15:32,250 --> 00:15:33,180
with many challenges.

333
00:15:33,180 --> 00:15:36,630
For example, it takes a long
time and costs a lot of money

334
00:15:36,630 --> 00:15:40,770
to train these big models,
and they cannot really be used

335
00:15:40,770 --> 00:15:45,060
by individuals who are not at
huge companies with hundreds

336
00:15:45,060 --> 00:15:48,570
or thousands of GPUs
and millions of dollars.

337
00:15:48,570 --> 00:15:52,290
So there's this challenge
called BabyLM or Baby Language

338
00:15:52,290 --> 00:15:54,360
Model, which is
attempting to train

339
00:15:54,360 --> 00:15:58,170
language models,
particularly smaller ones,

340
00:15:58,170 --> 00:16:02,550
on the same amount of linguistic
data available to a child.

341
00:16:02,550 --> 00:16:05,700
So data sets have grown by
orders of magnitude, as well

342
00:16:05,700 --> 00:16:07,650
as, of course, model size.

343
00:16:07,650 --> 00:16:11,460
For example, Chinchilla sees
approximately 1.4 trillion words

344
00:16:11,460 --> 00:16:12,240
during training.

345
00:16:12,240 --> 00:16:14,910
This is around 10,000 words
for every one word that

346
00:16:14,910 --> 00:16:18,750
a 13-year-old child, on average,
has heard as they grow up

347
00:16:18,750 --> 00:16:20,670
or develop.

348
00:16:20,670 --> 00:16:24,780
So the purpose here is,
can we close this gap?

349
00:16:24,780 --> 00:16:28,710
Can we train smaller models
on lower amounts of data

350
00:16:28,710 --> 00:16:33,090
while hopefully still attempting
to get the performance

351
00:16:33,090 --> 00:16:36,420
of these much larger models?

352
00:16:36,420 --> 00:16:38,130
So basically, we're
trying to focus

353
00:16:38,130 --> 00:16:42,030
on optimizing pre-training,
given data limitations inspired

354
00:16:42,030 --> 00:16:43,950
by human development.

355
00:16:43,950 --> 00:16:46,830
And this will also
ensure that research

356
00:16:46,830 --> 00:16:50,670
is possible for more
individuals, as well as labs,

357
00:16:50,670 --> 00:16:54,180
and potentially possible
on a university budget,

358
00:16:54,180 --> 00:16:56,190
as it seems now that
a lot of research

359
00:16:56,190 --> 00:16:59,160
is restricted to
large companies, which

360
00:16:59,160 --> 00:17:02,730
I said have a lot of
resources, as well as money.

361
00:17:02,730 --> 00:17:04,410
So again, why BabyLM?

362
00:17:04,410 --> 00:17:06,660
Well, it can greatly
improve the efficiency

363
00:17:06,660 --> 00:17:09,869
of training, as well as
using larger language models.

364
00:17:09,869 --> 00:17:15,109
It can potentially open up new
doors and potential use cases.

365
00:17:15,109 --> 00:17:17,119
It can lead to improved
interpretability,

366
00:17:17,119 --> 00:17:18,140
as well as alignment.

367
00:17:18,140 --> 00:17:21,170
Smaller models will be
easier to control, align,

368
00:17:21,170 --> 00:17:23,569
as well as interpret
what exactly is going on,

369
00:17:23,569 --> 00:17:27,260
compared to incredibly large
LLMs, which are basically

370
00:17:27,260 --> 00:17:28,880
huge black boxes.

371
00:17:28,880 --> 00:17:30,650
This will, again,
potentially lead

372
00:17:30,650 --> 00:17:32,720
to enhanced open-source
availability.

373
00:17:32,720 --> 00:17:37,730
For example, large language
models runnable on consumer PCs,

374
00:17:37,730 --> 00:17:41,000
as well as by smaller
labs and companies.

375
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,730
The techniques discovered
here can also possibly

376
00:17:43,730 --> 00:17:46,250
be applied to larger scales.

377
00:17:46,250 --> 00:17:48,980
And further, this may lead
to greater understanding

378
00:17:48,980 --> 00:17:50,630
of the cognitive
models of humans

379
00:17:50,630 --> 00:17:53,840
and how exactly we are able
to learn language much more

380
00:17:53,840 --> 00:17:56,660
efficiently than these
large language models.

381
00:17:56,660 --> 00:17:59,270
So there may be a flow of
knowledge from cognitive science

382
00:17:59,270 --> 00:18:01,520
and psychology to NLP
and machine learning,

383
00:18:01,520 --> 00:18:05,590
but also in the other direction.

384
00:18:05,590 --> 00:18:07,390
So briefly, the
BabyLM training data

385
00:18:07,390 --> 00:18:11,270
that the authors of
this challenge provide,

386
00:18:11,270 --> 00:18:15,860
it's a developmentally-inspired
pre-training data set,

387
00:18:15,860 --> 00:18:19,010
which has under 100 million
words, because children

388
00:18:19,010 --> 00:18:21,800
are exposed to approximately
2 to 7 million words per year

389
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:24,560
as they grow up, up
to the age of 13.

390
00:18:24,560 --> 00:18:26,330
That's approximately
90 million words.

391
00:18:26,330 --> 00:18:27,920
So they round up to 100.

392
00:18:27,920 --> 00:18:31,220
It's mostly transcribed speech,
and their motivation there

393
00:18:31,220 --> 00:18:34,460
is that most of the input
to children is spoken.

394
00:18:34,460 --> 00:18:37,700
And thus, their data set
focuses on transcribed speech.

395
00:18:37,700 --> 00:18:40,220
It's also mixed domain
because children are typically

396
00:18:40,220 --> 00:18:43,280
exposed to a variety
of language or speech

397
00:18:43,280 --> 00:18:46,160
from different domains.

398
00:18:46,160 --> 00:18:49,400
So it has child-directed
speech, OpenSubtitles,

399
00:18:49,400 --> 00:18:52,160
which are subtitles
of movies, TV shows

400
00:18:52,160 --> 00:18:56,210
and so forth, simple
children's books

401
00:18:56,210 --> 00:18:59,705
which contain stories
that children would likely

402
00:18:59,705 --> 00:19:00,830
hear as they're growing up.

403
00:19:00,830 --> 00:19:04,370
But it also has some Wikipedia,
as well as Simple Wikipedia.

404
00:19:04,370 --> 00:19:08,480
And here are just some examples
of child-directed speech,

405
00:19:08,480 --> 00:19:14,420
children's stories,
Wikipedia and so forth.

406
00:19:14,420 --> 00:19:16,880
So that's it for my portion
of the presentation.

407
00:19:16,880 --> 00:19:23,290
And I'll hand it off to Div, who
will talk a bit about AI agents.

408
00:19:23,290 --> 00:19:27,430

409
00:19:27,430 --> 00:19:29,190
Thanks.

410
00:19:29,190 --> 00:19:31,030
Yeah.

411
00:19:31,030 --> 00:19:33,040
So everyone must have seen--

412
00:19:33,040 --> 00:19:36,670
there's this new trend
where everything is

413
00:19:36,670 --> 00:19:37,960
transitioning to more agents.

414
00:19:37,960 --> 00:19:39,310
That's the new hot thing.

415
00:19:39,310 --> 00:19:41,070
And we're seeing this--

416
00:19:41,070 --> 00:19:42,820
people are going more
from language models

417
00:19:42,820 --> 00:19:44,500
to now building AI agents.

418
00:19:44,500 --> 00:19:46,240
And then what's the
biggest difference?

419
00:19:46,240 --> 00:19:48,610
Why agents?

420
00:19:48,610 --> 00:19:51,130
Why just not train a big
large language model?

421
00:19:51,130 --> 00:19:56,080
And I was sort of like go into
why, what's the difference?

422
00:19:56,080 --> 00:19:59,980
And then, also, discuss a
bunch of things such as,

423
00:19:59,980 --> 00:20:03,340
how can you use agents
for taking actions?

424
00:20:03,340 --> 00:20:05,590
What are some emergent
architectures?

425
00:20:05,590 --> 00:20:08,710
How can you build
human-like agents?

426
00:20:08,710 --> 00:20:11,020
How can you use it for
computer interactions?

427
00:20:11,020 --> 00:20:12,937
How do you solve problems
for long-term memory

428
00:20:12,937 --> 00:20:13,785
and personalization?

429
00:20:13,785 --> 00:20:15,160
And there's a lot
of other things

430
00:20:15,160 --> 00:20:17,440
you can do with
multi-agent communication.

431
00:20:17,440 --> 00:20:19,030
And there are some
future directions.

432
00:20:19,030 --> 00:20:22,180
So I'll try to cover
as much as I can.

433
00:20:22,180 --> 00:20:25,810
So first, let's talk about why
should we even build AI agents.

434
00:20:25,810 --> 00:20:30,850

435
00:20:30,850 --> 00:20:35,140
There's a key thesis, which is
that humans will communicate

436
00:20:35,140 --> 00:20:37,450
with AI using natural language.

437
00:20:37,450 --> 00:20:40,630
And AI will be operating
all the machines,

438
00:20:40,630 --> 00:20:43,450
thus allowing for more intuitive
and efficient operations.

439
00:20:43,450 --> 00:20:46,870
So right now, what happens
is, me, as a human,

440
00:20:46,870 --> 00:20:50,260
I'm directly using my
computer I'm using my phone,

441
00:20:50,260 --> 00:20:51,910
but this is really inefficient.

442
00:20:51,910 --> 00:20:54,790
We are not optimized by
nature to be able to do that.

443
00:20:54,790 --> 00:20:57,190
We are actually really,
really bad at this.

444
00:20:57,190 --> 00:21:01,150
But if you can just talk to our
AI, just like with language,

445
00:21:01,150 --> 00:21:03,820
and the AI is just really good
enough that it can just do this

446
00:21:03,820 --> 00:21:07,270
super faster, obviously like
100x speeds compared to human,

447
00:21:07,270 --> 00:21:08,410
and that's going to happen.

448
00:21:08,410 --> 00:21:10,630
And I think that's
the future of how

449
00:21:10,630 --> 00:21:13,600
things are going to evolve
in the next five years.

450
00:21:13,600 --> 00:21:16,457
And I call this Software 3.0.

451
00:21:16,457 --> 00:21:18,040
I have a blog post
about this that you

452
00:21:18,040 --> 00:21:20,470
can read if you want
to, where the idea is

453
00:21:20,470 --> 00:21:22,900
you can think of a
large language model

454
00:21:22,900 --> 00:21:25,510
as a computing chip in a sense.

455
00:21:25,510 --> 00:21:29,750
So it's similar to a chip
that's powering a whole system.

456
00:21:29,750 --> 00:21:32,480
And then you can build
abstractions and all.

457
00:21:32,480 --> 00:21:32,980
Cool.

458
00:21:32,980 --> 00:21:36,290

459
00:21:36,290 --> 00:21:38,690
So why do we need agents?

460
00:21:38,690 --> 00:21:41,180
So usually, a single call
to a large language model

461
00:21:41,180 --> 00:21:42,080
is not enough.

462
00:21:42,080 --> 00:21:42,830
You need chaining.

463
00:21:42,830 --> 00:21:44,060
You need recursion.

464
00:21:44,060 --> 00:21:45,380
You need a lot of more things.

465
00:21:45,380 --> 00:21:47,713
And that's where we want to
build systems, not just like

466
00:21:47,713 --> 00:21:49,790
a single monolith.

467
00:21:49,790 --> 00:21:52,230
Second is, yeah, so
how do we do this?

468
00:21:52,230 --> 00:21:55,220
So we do a lot of techniques,
especially around multiple calls

469
00:21:55,220 --> 00:21:55,992
to a model.

470
00:21:55,992 --> 00:21:57,950
And there's a lot of
ingredients involved here.

471
00:21:57,950 --> 00:22:00,620
And I will say,
building an agent is

472
00:22:00,620 --> 00:22:03,080
very similar to maybe thinking
about building a computer.

473
00:22:03,080 --> 00:22:05,540
So the LLM is like a CPU.

474
00:22:05,540 --> 00:22:06,290
So you have a CPU.

475
00:22:06,290 --> 00:22:08,660
But now you want to
solve the problems.

476
00:22:08,660 --> 00:22:09,740
How do you put ram?

477
00:22:09,740 --> 00:22:10,970
How do you put memory?

478
00:22:10,970 --> 00:22:12,590
How do you do actions?

479
00:22:12,590 --> 00:22:14,270
How do you build an interface?

480
00:22:14,270 --> 00:22:16,220
How do you add internet access?

481
00:22:16,220 --> 00:22:18,110
How do you personalize
it to the user?

482
00:22:18,110 --> 00:22:22,340
So this is almost like you are
trying to build a computer.

483
00:22:22,340 --> 00:22:26,210
And that's what makes it
a really hard problem.

484
00:22:26,210 --> 00:22:31,880
And this is like an example of a
general architecture for agents.

485
00:22:31,880 --> 00:22:36,840
This is from Lilian Weng,
who's a researcher at OpenAI.

486
00:22:36,840 --> 00:22:39,875
And you can imagine, an agent
has a lot of ingredients.

487
00:22:39,875 --> 00:22:41,250
So you want to
have memory, which

488
00:22:41,250 --> 00:22:42,690
would be short-term, long-term.

489
00:22:42,690 --> 00:22:44,250
You have tools,
which could be like,

490
00:22:44,250 --> 00:22:46,200
you can go and use
classical tools

491
00:22:46,200 --> 00:22:49,267
like a Calculator, Calendar,
Code Interpreter, et cetera.

492
00:22:49,267 --> 00:22:51,600
You want to have some sort
of like planning layer, where

493
00:22:51,600 --> 00:22:55,770
you can have Chain-of-Thoughts
and Trees of Thoughts,

494
00:22:55,770 --> 00:22:57,960
as Steven discussed,
and use all of that

495
00:22:57,960 --> 00:23:02,927
to actually act on behalf of
a user in some environment.

496
00:23:02,927 --> 00:23:06,410

497
00:23:06,410 --> 00:23:10,250
We'll go maybe discuss MULTION
a bit, just to give a sense.

498
00:23:10,250 --> 00:23:13,080
Also, the talk won't
be focused on that.

499
00:23:13,080 --> 00:23:15,260
So this is an agent
I'm building, which

500
00:23:15,260 --> 00:23:16,940
is more of a browser agent.

501
00:23:16,940 --> 00:23:19,400
The name is inspired from
Quantum Physics, specifically

502
