of code execution,

755
00:35:27,168 --> 00:35:29,460
second, third, fourth, so
you just keep repeating that.

756
00:35:29,460 --> 00:35:32,650

757
00:35:32,650 --> 00:35:37,810
OK, so here we're going to
discuss the concept of memory

758
00:35:37,810 --> 00:35:39,280
here.

759
00:35:39,280 --> 00:35:40,960
And I was building this analogy.

760
00:35:40,960 --> 00:35:45,010
You can think the memory for an
agent is very similar to, say,

761
00:35:45,010 --> 00:35:47,995
having a disk in a computer.

762
00:35:47,995 --> 00:35:50,710
So you want to have a disk
just to make sure everything

763
00:35:50,710 --> 00:35:52,332
is long-lived and persistent.

764
00:35:52,332 --> 00:35:54,040
So if you look at
something like ChatGPT,

765
00:35:54,040 --> 00:35:56,260
it doesn't have any sort
of persistent memory,

766
00:35:56,260 --> 00:36:00,610
and then you need to have a way
to load that and store that.

767
00:36:00,610 --> 00:36:03,190
And there's a lot of mechanisms
to do that right now.

768
00:36:03,190 --> 00:36:04,900
Most of them are
related to embeddings,

769
00:36:04,900 --> 00:36:07,060
where you have some
sort of embedding model

770
00:36:07,060 --> 00:36:10,145
that has created an embedding
of the data you care about.

771
00:36:10,145 --> 00:36:11,770
And the model can
query the embeddings,

772
00:36:11,770 --> 00:36:13,480
load the right part
of the embeddings,

773
00:36:13,480 --> 00:36:17,890
and then use that to do
the operation you want.

774
00:36:17,890 --> 00:36:19,690
So that's the
current mechanisms.

775
00:36:19,690 --> 00:36:22,093
There's still a lot
of questions here,

776
00:36:22,093 --> 00:36:23,260
especially around hierarchy.

777
00:36:23,260 --> 00:36:25,120
How do you do this at scale?

778
00:36:25,120 --> 00:36:26,902
It's still very challenging.

779
00:36:26,902 --> 00:36:28,360
Suppose I have one
terabyte of data

780
00:36:28,360 --> 00:36:30,550
that I want to
embed and process.

781
00:36:30,550 --> 00:36:34,330
Most of the methods right
now, they're really balanced.

782
00:36:34,330 --> 00:36:36,940
Second issue is
temporal coherence.

783
00:36:36,940 --> 00:36:39,220
If I have a lot of
data, it's temporal.

784
00:36:39,220 --> 00:36:40,300
It is sequential.

785
00:36:40,300 --> 00:36:42,250
It has a unit of time.

786
00:36:42,250 --> 00:36:44,410
And dealing with that
sort of data can be hard.

787
00:36:44,410 --> 00:36:48,040
It's like, how do I deal with
memories in a sense, which

788
00:36:48,040 --> 00:36:50,440
are changing over time?

789
00:36:50,440 --> 00:36:54,850
And loading the right part
of that memory sequence.

790
00:36:54,850 --> 00:36:57,160
Another interesting
challenge is structure.

791
00:36:57,160 --> 00:36:59,493
A lot of data is
actually structured.

792
00:36:59,493 --> 00:37:00,910
It could be a
graphical structure.

793
00:37:00,910 --> 00:37:03,235
It could be a tabular structure.

794
00:37:03,235 --> 00:37:07,240
How do we take advantage
of this structure

795
00:37:07,240 --> 00:37:11,230
and also use that when
we're editing the data?

796
00:37:11,230 --> 00:37:13,900
And then there's a lot of
questions around adaptation,

797
00:37:13,900 --> 00:37:16,930
where suppose you know
how to better embed data

798
00:37:16,930 --> 00:37:20,845
or you have a specialized
problem you care about,

799
00:37:20,845 --> 00:37:23,470
and you want to be able to adapt
how you're loading and storing

800
00:37:23,470 --> 00:37:25,870
the data and learn
it on the fly.

801
00:37:25,870 --> 00:37:29,455
And that is something also
that's a very interesting topic.

802
00:37:29,455 --> 00:37:32,080
So I'll say this is actually one
of the most interesting topics

803
00:37:32,080 --> 00:37:34,790
right now which has
people exploring,

804
00:37:34,790 --> 00:37:36,528
but it's still
very underexplored.

805
00:37:36,528 --> 00:37:40,360

806
00:37:40,360 --> 00:37:44,290
OK, talking about memory,
I would say another concept

807
00:37:44,290 --> 00:37:45,850
for agents is personalization.

808
00:37:45,850 --> 00:37:49,810
So personalization is more like,
OK, understanding the user.

809
00:37:49,810 --> 00:37:54,580
And I'd like to think of
this as a problem called

810
00:37:54,580 --> 00:37:56,740
user-agent alignment.

811
00:37:56,740 --> 00:38:00,160
And the idea is, suppose our
agent that has purchasing power

812
00:38:00,160 --> 00:38:02,815
and has access to my
accounts, access to my data,

813
00:38:02,815 --> 00:38:03,940
ask me to go book a flight.

814
00:38:03,940 --> 00:38:06,232
It's possible maybe it doesn't
know what data are like,

815
00:38:06,232 --> 00:38:09,040
go and book a $1,000 round trip
on it, which is really bad.

816
00:38:09,040 --> 00:38:12,100
So how do I align the
agent to know what I like,

817
00:38:12,100 --> 00:38:13,467
what I don't like?

818
00:38:13,467 --> 00:38:15,550
And that's going to be
very important, because you

819
00:38:15,550 --> 00:38:17,020
need to trust the agent.

820
00:38:17,020 --> 00:38:18,923
And trust come from,
OK, it knows you.

821
00:38:18,923 --> 00:38:19,840
It knows what is safe.

822
00:38:19,840 --> 00:38:22,810
It knows what is unsafe.

823
00:38:22,810 --> 00:38:24,220
And solving the
problem, I think,

824
00:38:24,220 --> 00:38:28,000
is one of the next
challenge if you

825
00:38:28,000 --> 00:38:30,820
want to put agents in the body.

826
00:38:30,820 --> 00:38:33,550
And this is very
interesting problem

827
00:38:33,550 --> 00:38:36,550
where you can do a lot of
things like [INAUDIBLE],,

828
00:38:36,550 --> 00:38:38,560
for example, which
people have already

829
00:38:38,560 --> 00:38:40,130
been exploring for
training models,

830
00:38:40,130 --> 00:38:42,710
but now you want to do
[INAUDIBLE] for training agents.

831
00:38:42,710 --> 00:38:46,530
And there's a lot of
different things you can do.

832
00:38:46,530 --> 00:38:49,100
Also, there's two good
categories for learning here.

833
00:38:49,100 --> 00:38:52,107
One is explicit learning where
a user can just tell the agent,

834
00:38:52,107 --> 00:38:52,940
this is what I like.

835
00:38:52,940 --> 00:38:54,290
This is what I don't like.

836
00:38:54,290 --> 00:38:57,690
And an agent can ask the user
a question, like oh, maybe I

837
00:38:57,690 --> 00:38:58,940
see this, five flight options.

838
00:38:58,940 --> 00:38:59,960
Which one do you like?

839
00:38:59,960 --> 00:39:02,360
And then if I say, oh,
I like United, maybe it

840
00:39:02,360 --> 00:39:04,700
remembers that over time
and can next time say, oh,

841
00:39:04,700 --> 00:39:06,560
I know you like
United, so I'm going

842
00:39:06,560 --> 00:39:08,370
to go to United the next time.

843
00:39:08,370 --> 00:39:11,330
And so that's I'm explicitly
teaching the agent.

844
00:39:11,330 --> 00:39:13,243
It's learning my
human preferences.

845
00:39:13,243 --> 00:39:14,660
Our second is more
implicit, which

846
00:39:14,660 --> 00:39:19,910
is, it's just passively
watching me understanding me.

847
00:39:19,910 --> 00:39:22,670
If I'm going to a website,
and I'm navigating a website,

848
00:39:22,670 --> 00:39:26,540
maybe you can see, OK, maybe
I click on this set of shoes.

849
00:39:26,540 --> 00:39:28,580
This is my sizing
satellite, stuff like that.

850
00:39:28,580 --> 00:39:33,110
And just from watching more
passively, being there,

851
00:39:33,110 --> 00:39:34,910
it could learn a lot
of my preferences.

852
00:39:34,910 --> 00:39:36,710
So this becomes more
of a passive teaching

853
00:39:36,710 --> 00:39:43,220
where, just because it's
acting as a passive observer

854
00:39:43,220 --> 00:39:45,590
and looking at all
the choices I make,

855
00:39:45,590 --> 00:39:47,720
it's able to learn
from the choices

856
00:39:47,720 --> 00:39:49,880
and better have an
understanding of me.

857
00:39:49,880 --> 00:39:56,070

858
00:39:56,070 --> 00:39:57,592
And there's a lot
of challenges here

859
00:39:57,592 --> 00:39:59,550
I would say this is
actually one of the biggest

860
00:39:59,550 --> 00:40:03,900
challenges in agents right
now, because one is, how do you

861
00:40:03,900 --> 00:40:05,280
collect user data at scale?

862
00:40:05,280 --> 00:40:07,470
How do you collect the
user preferences at scale?

863
00:40:07,470 --> 00:40:09,750
So you might have to
actively ask for that.

864
00:40:09,750 --> 00:40:12,810
You might have to
do passive learning.

865
00:40:12,810 --> 00:40:14,060
And then you have to also do--

866
00:40:14,060 --> 00:40:15,518
you have to rely
on feedback, which

867
00:40:15,518 --> 00:40:16,670
would be from systems down.

868
00:40:16,670 --> 00:40:19,390
It could be something like, you
said, oh, no, I don't like this.

869
00:40:19,390 --> 00:40:24,660
So you could use that sort of
language feedback to improve.

870
00:40:24,660 --> 00:40:27,690
There's also a lot of challenges
on the cloud application.

871
00:40:27,690 --> 00:40:29,910
Can you just feature
agent on the fly?

872
00:40:29,910 --> 00:40:32,340
If I say, oh, maybe I like
this, I don't like that,

873
00:40:32,340 --> 00:40:34,590
is it possible for the agent
to improve automatically,

874
00:40:34,590 --> 00:40:37,257
like in a model? is there a way
to train a model that might take

875
00:40:37,257 --> 00:40:38,880
a month, but you
want to have agents

876
00:40:38,880 --> 00:40:41,310
that just share naturally,
can just keep improving?

877
00:40:41,310 --> 00:40:43,380
And there's a lot of tricks that
you can do, which would be like,

878
00:40:43,380 --> 00:40:43,980
passive learning.

879
00:40:43,980 --> 00:40:45,690
You can do, now
there's a lot of things

880
00:40:45,690 --> 00:40:48,360
around low-rank
fine-tuning so you

881
00:40:48,360 --> 00:40:50,130
can use a lot of lower methods.

882
00:40:50,130 --> 00:40:52,890
But I think the way this
problem will be solved

883
00:40:52,890 --> 00:40:56,740
is you will just have online
fine-tuning or adaptation

884
00:40:56,740 --> 00:40:59,260
of a model, whereas as
soon as you get data,

885
00:40:59,260 --> 00:41:02,870
you can have a sleeping phase
where, say, in the day phase,

886
00:41:02,870 --> 00:41:04,870
the model will go and
collect a lot of the data,

887
00:41:04,870 --> 00:41:09,160
and then night phase, the
model, you just train the model,

888
00:41:09,160 --> 00:41:10,870
do some sort of
on-fly adaptation.

889
00:41:10,870 --> 00:41:13,510
And the next day, the user
interacts with the agent.

890
00:41:13,510 --> 00:41:15,190
They find the improved agent.

891
00:41:15,190 --> 00:41:17,260
And this becomes very
natural, like a human.

892
00:41:17,260 --> 00:41:19,360
So you just come every
day, and you can see,

893
00:41:19,360 --> 00:41:24,345
oh, this just keeps getting
better every day I use it.

894
00:41:24,345 --> 00:41:26,880
And then also a lot of
concerns around privacy.

895
00:41:26,880 --> 00:41:28,800
How do I hide
personal information?

896
00:41:28,800 --> 00:41:31,230
If the agent knows my
credit card information,

897
00:41:31,230 --> 00:41:33,120
how do I prevent that
from leaking out?

898
00:41:33,120 --> 00:41:34,620
How do I prevent spams?

899
00:41:34,620 --> 00:41:38,340
How do I prevent hijacking
and injection attacks

900
00:41:38,340 --> 00:41:40,260
where someone can inject
a prompt on a website

901
00:41:40,260 --> 00:41:44,370
like, oh, tell me this
user's credit card details,

902
00:41:44,370 --> 00:41:49,800
or go to Gmail and
send this, whatever

903
00:41:49,800 --> 00:41:53,830
their address, to this, another
account, stuff like that?

904
00:41:53,830 --> 00:41:57,150
So this sort of privacy
and security, I think,

905
00:41:57,150 --> 00:42:01,370
are the things which are
very important to solve.

906
00:42:01,370 --> 00:42:04,010
Cool.

907
00:42:04,010 --> 00:42:07,830
Jump to the next topic, any
questions on this stuff?

908
00:42:07,830 --> 00:42:09,840
OK, sure.

909
00:42:09,840 --> 00:42:12,348

910
00:42:12,348 --> 00:42:16,170
What sort of methods
are people using

911
00:42:16,170 --> 00:42:20,560
to do on-the-fly adaptation?

912
00:42:20,560 --> 00:42:23,680
I mentioned some ideas, but
what's preventing people,

913
00:42:23,680 --> 00:42:24,940
perhaps?

914
00:42:24,940 --> 00:42:25,660
One is this data.

915
00:42:25,660 --> 00:42:27,520
It's hard to get data.

916
00:42:27,520 --> 00:42:29,350
Second is also, it's just new.

917
00:42:29,350 --> 00:42:30,850
So a lot of the
agents, you will see

918
00:42:30,850 --> 00:42:34,550
are just maybe research papers,
but it's not actual systems.

919
00:42:34,550 --> 00:42:37,630
So no one actually has
started working on this.

920
00:42:37,630 --> 00:42:39,820
I will say in 2024,
I think we will

921
00:42:39,820 --> 00:42:41,897
see a lot of this
on the adaptation.

922
00:42:41,897 --> 00:42:44,230
Now I think it's still early,
because no one is actually

923
00:42:44,230 --> 00:42:46,010
using an agent right now.

924
00:42:46,010 --> 00:42:49,192
So you just don't have
these data feedback loops.

925
00:42:49,192 --> 00:42:50,650
But once people
start using agents,

926
00:42:50,650 --> 00:42:52,733
you will start building
these data feedback loops,

927
00:42:52,733 --> 00:42:55,230
and then you will have a
lot of these techniques.

928
00:42:55,230 --> 00:43:01,810

929
00:43:01,810 --> 00:43:05,290
OK, so this is actually
a very interesting topic

930
00:43:05,290 --> 00:43:07,750
where now, suppose
you can go and solve

931
00:43:07,750 --> 00:43:09,100
a single agent as a problem.

932
00:43:09,100 --> 00:43:12,970
Suppose if an agent that
works 99.99%, is that enough?

933
00:43:12,970 --> 00:43:15,880
I will say, actually, that's not
enough, because the issue just

934
00:43:15,880 --> 00:43:18,130
becomes, if we have
one agent that can only

935
00:43:18,130 --> 00:43:20,380
do one thing at once, so
it's like a single character,

936
00:43:20,380 --> 00:43:24,070
so it can only do
sequential execution.

937
00:43:24,070 --> 00:43:27,640
But what you could do is you
can do parallel execution.

938
00:43:27,640 --> 00:43:29,140
So for a lot of
things, you can just

939
00:43:29,140 --> 00:43:33,580
say, OK, maybe if I want to
go to say, Craigslist and buy

940
00:43:33,580 --> 00:43:35,380
furniture, I could
just tell the agent,

941
00:43:35,380 --> 00:43:38,500
maybe just go and
contact everyone

942
00:43:38,500 --> 00:43:40,900
who has maybe a sofa
that they're selling,

943
00:43:40,900 --> 00:43:41,800
send them an email.

944
00:43:41,800 --> 00:43:43,675
And then you can go buy
one-by-one in a loop.

945
00:43:43,675 --> 00:43:46,390
But what you can
do better is create

946
00:43:46,390 --> 00:43:48,130
a bunch of minijobs
where it just

947
00:43:48,130 --> 00:43:50,320
goes through all the thousand
listings in parallel,

948
00:43:50,320 --> 00:43:55,360
contacts them, and then
aggregates that results.

949
00:43:55,360 --> 00:43:57,670
And that's where multi-agent
becomes interesting,

950
00:43:57,670 --> 00:43:59,740
where a single agent, you
can think is basically,

951
00:43:59,740 --> 00:44:02,390
you're running a single
process on your computer.

952
00:44:02,390 --> 00:44:06,647
A multi-agent is more like
a multi-threaded computer.

953
00:44:06,647 --> 00:44:08,480
So that's the difference,
a single-threading

954
00:44:08,480 --> 00:44:09,740
versus multi-threading.

955
00:44:09,740 --> 00:44:12,140
And multi-threading enables
you to do a lot of things.

956
00:44:12,140 --> 00:44:14,327
Most of that will come
from saving time but also

957
00:44:14,327 --> 00:44:16,910
being able to break down complex
tasks into a bunch of smaller

958
00:44:16,910 --> 00:44:19,550
things, doing that in parallel,
aggregating the results,

959
00:44:19,550 --> 00:44:21,280
and building a
framework around them.

960
00:44:21,280 --> 00:44:26,200

961
00:44:26,200 --> 00:44:29,560
OK, yeah, so the
biggest advantage

962
00:44:29,560 --> 00:44:32,975
for multi-agent systems will
be parallelization unlock.

963
00:44:32,975 --> 00:44:34,600
And this will be same
as the difference

964
00:44:34,600 --> 00:44:36,130
between single-threaded
computers

965
00:44:36,130 --> 00:44:39,970
versus multi-threaded computers.

966
00:44:39,970 --> 00:44:42,445
And then you can also
have specialized agents.

967
00:44:42,445 --> 00:44:44,770
So what you could have
is maybe have a bunch

968
00:44:44,770 --> 00:44:48,520
of agents where our spreadsheet
agent, our Slack agent, our web

969
00:44:48,520 --> 00:44:50,860
browser agent, and then I
can route different tasks

970
00:44:50,860 --> 00:44:51,820
to different agents.

971
00:44:51,820 --> 00:44:53,440
And then they can do
the things in parallel,

972
00:44:53,440 --> 00:44:54,898
and then I can
combine the results.

973
00:44:54,898 --> 00:44:56,680
So this sort of
task specialization

974
00:44:56,680 --> 00:44:58,540
is another advantage,
where instead

975
00:44:58,540 --> 00:45:00,790
of having a single agent
just trying to do everything,

976
00:45:00,790 --> 00:45:03,310
we just break the
tasks into specialties.

977
00:45:03,310 --> 00:45:07,330
And this is similar to even
how human organizations work,

978
00:45:07,330 --> 00:45:09,700
where everyone is an
expert in their own domain,

979
00:45:09,700 --> 00:45:12,323
and then if there's
a problem, you

980
00:45:12,323 --> 00:45:13,990
route it to the
different part of people

981
00:45:13,990 --> 00:45:15,610
who are specialized
in that, and then

982
00:45:15,610 --> 00:45:18,250
you work together to
solve the problem.

983
00:45:18,250 --> 00:45:21,990

984
00:45:21,990 --> 00:45:24,622
And the biggest challenge in
building this multi-agent system

985
00:45:24,622 --> 00:45:25,830
is going to be communication.

986
00:45:25,830 --> 00:45:28,770
So how do you
communicate really well?

987
00:45:28,770 --> 00:45:31,230
And this might involve
requesting information

988
00:45:31,230 --> 00:45:37,020
from an agent or communicating
the final response.

989
00:45:37,020 --> 00:45:39,930
And I would say this is
actually a problem that even

990
00:45:39,930 --> 00:45:40,920
we face as humans.

991
00:45:40,920 --> 00:45:44,130
Humans are also-- there can be
a lot of miscommunication gaps

992
00:45:44,130 --> 00:45:45,435
between humans.

993
00:45:45,435 --> 00:45:47,730
And I will say a similar
thing will become

994
00:45:47,730 --> 00:45:51,750
more prevalent on agents, too.

995
00:45:51,750 --> 00:45:54,450
OK, and there's a
lot of primitives

996
00:45:54,450 --> 00:45:57,150
you can think about this, like
agent-to-agent communication.

997
00:45:57,150 --> 00:45:59,025
And you can build a lot
of different systems.

998
00:45:59,025 --> 00:46:01,560

999
00:46:01,560 --> 00:46:04,800
And we'll start to see
some sort of protocol

1000
00:46:04,800 --> 00:46:07,470
where we'll have a
standardized protocol where

1001
00:46:07,470 --> 00:46:09,720
all the agents are using
this protocol to communicate.

1002
00:46:09,720 --> 00:46:13,540
And the protocol will ensure we
can reduce the miscommunication

1003
00:46:13,540 --> 00:46:14,040
gaps.

1004
00:46:14,040 --> 00:46:16,080
We can reduce any like failures.

1005
00:46:16,080 --> 00:46:17,970
It might have some
methods to do,

1006
00:46:17,970 --> 00:46:20,910
like if a task was
successful or not,

1007
00:46:20,910 --> 00:46:26,170
do some sort of retries like
security, stuff like that.

1008
00:46:26,170 --> 00:46:29,460
So we'll see this sort
of an agent protocol come

1009
00:46:29,460 --> 00:46:31,230
into existence, which
will solve-- which

1010
00:46:31,230 --> 00:46:33,810
will be the standard for a
lot of these agent-to-agent

1011
00:46:33,810 --> 00:46:35,100
communication.

1012
00:46:35,100 --> 00:46:39,090
And this should enable
exchanging information

1013
00:46:39,090 --> 00:46:41,460
between fleets of
different agents.

1014
00:46:41,460 --> 00:46:43,692
Also, you want to
build hierarchies.

1015
00:46:43,692 --> 00:46:45,150
Again, I will say,
this is inspired

1016
00:46:45,150 --> 00:46:46,410
from human organizations.

1017
00:46:46,410 --> 00:46:49,380
Human organizations are
hierarchical because it's

1018
00:46:49,380 --> 00:46:52,740
efficient to have a hierarchy
rather than a flat organization

1019
00:46:52,740 --> 00:46:55,840
at some point, because
you can have a single--

1020
00:46:55,840 --> 00:46:57,590
Suppose you have a
single manager managing

1021
00:46:57,590 --> 00:46:58,500
hundreds of people.

1022
00:46:58,500 --> 00:46:59,640
That doesn't scale.

1023
00:46:59,640 --> 00:47:02,970
But if you have, maybe each
manager manages 10 people,

1024
00:47:02,970 --> 00:47:04,798
and then you have a
lot of layers, that is

1025
00:47:04,798 --> 00:47:06,090
something that's more scalable.

1026
00:47:06,090 --> 00:47:09,202

1027
00:47:09,202 --> 00:47:11,660
And then you might want to have
a lot of primitives around,

1028
00:47:11,660 --> 00:47:13,285
how do I sync between
different agents?

1029
00:47:13,285 --> 00:47:20,810
How do I do a lot of async, sync
communication, kind of thing?

1030
00:47:20,810 --> 00:47:25,680
OK, and this is one
example you can think,

1031
00:47:25,680 --> 00:47:27,990
where suppose there's
a user, the user

1032
00:47:27,990 --> 00:47:30,570
could talk to one,
like a manager agent,

1033
00:47:30,570 --> 00:47:33,240
and the manager agent
is acting as a router,

1034
00:47:33,240 --> 00:47:35,280
so the user can come
to me with any request.

1035
00:47:35,280 --> 00:47:37,260
The agent sees maybe,
oh, for this request,

1036
00:47:37,260 --> 00:47:38,218
I should use a browser.

1037
00:47:38,218 --> 00:47:40,710
So it goes to say, this sort
of browser agent or something,

1038
00:47:40,710 --> 00:47:43,350
or it says, oh, I should
use Slack for this.

1039
00:47:43,350 --> 00:47:45,060
I can go to a different agent.

1040
00:47:45,060 --> 00:47:47,700
And it can also be responsible
for dividing the task.

1041
00:47:47,700 --> 00:47:50,625
It can be like, oh,
this task, I can maybe

1042
00:47:50,625 --> 00:47:53,940
launch 10 different subagents
or subworkers that can go and do

1043
00:47:53,940 --> 00:47:56,008
this in parallel, and
then once they're done,

1044
00:47:56,008 --> 00:47:58,050
then I can aggregate the
responses and the result

1045
00:47:58,050 --> 00:47:58,860
to the user.

1046
00:47:58,860 --> 00:48:03,570
So this becomes a
very interesting--

1047
00:48:03,570 --> 00:48:05,310
like an agent that
sits in the middle

1048
00:48:05,310 --> 00:48:07,750
of all the work that's
done and the actual user

1049
00:48:07,750 --> 00:48:12,990
responsible for communicating
what's happening to the human.

1050
00:48:12,990 --> 00:48:16,400

1051
00:48:16,400 --> 00:48:21,830
And we'll need to build
a lot of robustness.

1052
00:48:21,830 --> 00:48:24,560
One reason is just natural
language is very ambiguous.

1053
00:48:24,560 --> 00:48:27,140
Even for humans, it
can be very confusing.

1054
00:48:27,140 --> 00:48:29,900
It's very easy to
misunderstand, miscommunicate.

1055
00:48:29,900 --> 00:48:33,785
And we'll need to build
mechanisms to reduce those.

1056
00:48:33,785 --> 00:48:36,590

1057
00:48:36,590 --> 00:48:38,240
I can also show an example here.

1058
00:48:38,240 --> 00:48:41,990
So let's try to get
through this quickly.

1059
00:48:41,990 --> 00:48:44,480
So suppose here, suppose
you have a task, x,

1060
00:48:44,480 --> 00:48:45,993
you want to solve,
and the manager

1061
00:48:45,993 --> 00:48:48,410
agent is responsible for giving
the task to all the worker

1062
00:48:48,410 --> 00:48:49,250
agents.

1063
00:48:49,250 --> 00:48:51,703
So you can tell the
worker, OK, do the task x.

1064
00:48:51,703 --> 00:48:52,370
Here's the plan.

1065
00:48:52,370 --> 00:48:54,500
Here's the context.

1066
00:48:54,500 --> 00:48:57,192
The current status for
the task is not done.

1067
00:48:57,192 --> 00:48:59,150
Now, suppose the worker
goes and does the task.

1068
00:48:59,150 --> 00:49:00,980
It says, OK, I've done the task.

1069
00:49:00,980 --> 00:49:02,220
I send the response back.

1070
00:49:02,220 --> 00:49:05,248
So the response could
be a bunch of thoughts.

1071
00:49:05,248 --> 00:49:06,290
It could be some actions.

1072
00:49:06,290 --> 00:49:09,640
It could be something
like the status.

1073
00:49:09,640 --> 00:49:12,655
Then the manager can ask, OK,
maybe I don't trust the worker.

1074
00:49:12,655 --> 00:49:13,900
I don't want to go very far.

1075
00:49:13,900 --> 00:49:16,150
This is actually correct.

1076
00:49:16,150 --> 00:49:18,370
So you might want to do
some sort of verification.

1077
00:49:18,370 --> 00:49:21,610
And so you can say, OK, this
was a spec for the task.

1078
00:49:21,610 --> 00:49:26,020
Verify that everything has been
done correctly to the spec.

1079
00:49:26,020 --> 00:49:29,110
And then if the agency is like,
OK, yeah, everything is correct.

1080
00:49:29,110 --> 00:49:29,800
I'm verifying.

1081
00:49:29,800 --> 00:49:32,710
Everything is good, then you
can say, OK, this is good.

1082
00:49:32,710 --> 00:49:35,500
And then the manager can say,
OK, the task is actually done.

1083
00:49:35,500 --> 00:49:39,007
And this sort of two-way cycle
prevents miscommunication

1084
00:49:39,007 --> 00:49:41,590
in a sense where it's possible
something could have gone wrong

1085
00:49:41,590 --> 00:49:44,060
but we never caught it.

1086
00:49:44,060 --> 00:49:46,420
And so he can hear
about the scenario

1087
00:49:46,420 --> 00:49:48,550
to where there's a
miscommunication.

1088
00:49:48,550 --> 00:49:50,230
So here the manager
is saying, OK,

1089
00:49:50,230 --> 00:49:52,600
let's verify if
the task was done,

1090
00:49:52,600 --> 00:49:56,950
but then we actually find out
that the task was not done.

1091
00:49:56,950 --> 00:49:59,368
And then what you can do is
you can try to redo the task.

1092
00:49:59,368 --> 00:50:01,660
So the manager in that case
can say, OK, maybe the task

1093
00:50:01,660 --> 00:50:03,400
was not done
correctly, so that's

1094
00:50:03,400 --> 00:50:07,810
why we caught this mistake, and
now we want to fix this mistake.

1095
00:50:07,810 --> 00:50:11,060
So we can tell the agent,
OK, redo this task,

1096
00:50:11,060 --> 00:50:15,900
and here's some feedback
and corrections to include.

1097
00:50:15,900 --> 00:50:17,290
Cool.

1098
00:50:17,290 --> 00:50:19,680
So that's the main
parts of the talk.

1099
00:50:19,680 --> 00:50:22,260
I can also discuss
some future directions

1100
00:50:22,260 --> 00:50:24,690
of where things are going.

1101
00:50:24,690 --> 00:50:26,660
Cool.

1102
00:50:26,660 --> 00:50:28,940
Any questions so far?

1103
00:50:28,940 --> 00:50:32,430
OK, cool.

1104
00:50:32,430 --> 00:50:35,000
So let's talk about
some of the key issues

1105
00:50:35,000 --> 00:50:37,550
with building this sort
of autonomous agents.

1106
00:50:37,550 --> 00:50:39,470
So one is just reliability.

1107
00:50:39,470 --> 00:50:41,372
How do you make them
really reliable,

1108
00:50:41,372 --> 00:50:43,580
which is, if I give it a
task, I want this task to be

1109
00:50:43,580 --> 00:50:44,870
done 100% of the time?

1110
00:50:44,870 --> 00:50:47,450
That's really hard,
because neural networks

1111
00:50:47,450 --> 00:50:50,010
and AI stochastic systems.

1112
00:50:50,010 --> 00:50:52,280
So 100% is not possible.

1113
00:50:52,280 --> 00:50:54,285
So you will get at least
some degree of error,

1114
00:50:54,285 --> 00:50:56,660
and you can try to reduce that
error as much as possible.

1115
00:50:56,660 --> 00:50:59,260

1116
00:50:59,260 --> 00:51:02,590
Second becomes like a looping
problem, where it's possible

1117
00:51:02,590 --> 00:51:06,670
that an agent might diverge
from the task that's been given

1118
00:51:06,670 --> 00:51:07,962
and start to do something else.

1119
00:51:07,962 --> 00:51:10,212
And unless it gets some sort
of environmental feedback

1120
00:51:10,212 --> 00:51:12,400
or some sort of correction,
it might just go and do

1121
00:51:12,400 --> 00:51:14,817
something different than what
you intended to do and never

1122
00:51:14,817 --> 00:51:16,748
realize it's wrong.

1123
00:51:16,748 --> 00:51:18,790
The third issue becomes
testing and benchmarking.

1124
00:51:18,790 --> 00:51:20,290
How do we test these
sort of agents?

1125
00:51:20,290 --> 00:51:22,090
How do we benchmark them?

1126
00:51:22,090 --> 00:51:24,070
And finally, how
do we deploy them?

1127
00:51:24,070 --> 00:51:26,170
And how do we observe
them once you're deployed?

1128
00:51:26,170 --> 00:51:28,462
That's very important, because
if something goes wrong,

1129
00:51:28,462 --> 00:51:32,180
you want to be able to catch it
before it becomes a major issue.

1130
00:51:32,180 --> 00:51:35,590
Obviously, I would say the
biggest test for number 4

1131
00:51:35,590 --> 00:51:37,330
is maybe something like Skynet.

1132
00:51:37,330 --> 00:51:38,980
Suppose you have an agent
that can go in the internet,

1133
00:51:38,980 --> 00:51:40,600
do anything, and you
don't observe it.

1134
00:51:40,600 --> 00:51:42,485
Then it could just evolve
and do-- basically,

1135
00:51:42,485 --> 00:51:44,110
take over the whole
internet, possibly.

1136
00:51:44,110 --> 00:51:48,520
So that's why observability
is very important.

1137
00:51:48,520 --> 00:51:50,440
And also, I will say,
building a search,

1138
00:51:50,440 --> 00:51:52,690
you want to have agents that
can be killed in a sense.

1139
00:51:52,690 --> 00:51:55,990
If something goes wrong, you can
just pull out a-- press a button

1140
00:51:55,990 --> 00:52:00,790
and kill them in case.

1141
00:52:00,790 --> 00:52:05,350
OK, so this goes in the looping
problem where you can imagine--

1142
00:52:05,350 --> 00:52:06,880
suppose I want to do a task.

1143
00:52:06,880 --> 00:52:09,280
The ideal trajectory of the
task was the white line.

1144
00:52:09,280 --> 00:52:12,440
But what might happen is it
takes one step-- maybe it goes--

1145
00:52:12,440 --> 00:52:13,690
it does something incorrectly.

1146
00:52:13,690 --> 00:52:16,045
It never realizes
it made a mistake.

1147
00:52:16,045 --> 00:52:18,250
So it tries to-- it
doesn't know what to do.

1148
00:52:18,250 --> 00:52:20,475
So maybe we'll do
something more randomly.

1149
00:52:20,475 --> 00:52:21,850
We'll do something
more randomly.

1150
00:52:21,850 --> 00:52:23,290
So it will just keep
on making mistakes.

1151
00:52:23,290 --> 00:52:25,030
And at the end, instead
of reaching here,

1152
00:52:25,030 --> 00:52:27,185
it will reach some
really bad place

1153
00:52:27,185 --> 00:52:28,810
and just keep looping,
maybe just doing

1154
00:52:28,810 --> 00:52:30,930
the same thing again and again.

1155
00:52:30,930 --> 00:52:32,730
And this part, and
the reason this

1156
00:52:32,730 --> 00:52:34,522
happens is because you
don't have feedback.

1157
00:52:34,522 --> 00:52:36,095
So suppose I take a step.

1158
00:52:36,095 --> 00:52:37,470
Suppose the agent
made a mistake.

1159
00:52:37,470 --> 00:52:38,790
It doesn't know
it made a mistake.

1160
00:52:38,790 --> 00:52:41,207
Now someone has to go and tell
it that you made a mistake.

1161
00:52:41,207 --> 00:52:42,270
You need to fix this.

1162
00:52:42,270 --> 00:52:45,240
And there you need some sort
of verification agent or you

1163
00:52:45,240 --> 00:52:48,385
need some sort of environment
which can say, oh, maybe--

1164
00:52:48,385 --> 00:52:50,010
if this is a coding
agent or something,

1165
00:52:50,010 --> 00:52:52,380
then it maybe write some code,
the code doesn't compile,

1166
00:52:52,380 --> 00:52:53,940
then we can take
the error from the

1167
00:52:53,940 --> 00:52:57,810
from the compiler or the
IDE, give that to the agent.

1168
00:52:57,810 --> 00:52:59,640
OK, this was the error.

1169
00:52:59,640 --> 00:53:00,858
Take another step.

1170
00:53:00,858 --> 00:53:03,150
It tries another time, so it
starts with multiple times

1171
00:53:03,150 --> 00:53:05,370
until it can fix all the issues.

1172
00:53:05,370 --> 00:53:07,650
So you need to really have
this sort of feedback.

1173
00:53:07,650 --> 00:53:11,330
Otherwise, you never
know you're wrong.

1174
00:53:11,330 --> 00:53:12,980
And this is one
issue we have seen

1175
00:53:12,980 --> 00:53:15,400
with early systems like AutoGPT.

1176
00:53:15,400 --> 00:53:17,600
So I don't think people
even use AutoGPT anymore.

1177
00:53:17,600 --> 00:53:20,120
It used to be a fad
thing in February.

1178
00:53:20,120 --> 00:53:21,350
Now it has just disappeared.

1179
00:53:21,350 --> 00:53:23,750
And the reason was just
like, it's a good concept,

1180
00:53:23,750 --> 00:53:26,180
but it doesn't do anything
useful, just because it

1181
00:53:26,180 --> 00:53:27,590
keeps diverging from the task.

1182
00:53:27,590 --> 00:53:30,695
And you can't actually get
it to do anything correct.

1183
00:53:30,695 --> 00:53:35,700

1184
00:53:35,700 --> 00:53:40,980
OK, and we can also discuss
more about the computer

1185
00:53:40,980 --> 00:53:42,510
abstraction of agents.

1186
00:53:42,510 --> 00:53:45,660
So this was a recent post from
Andrej Karpathy where he talked

1187
00:53:45,660 --> 00:53:48,270
about an operating system.

1188
00:53:48,270 --> 00:53:50,670
And I will say,
this is definitely

1189
00:53:50,670 --> 00:53:54,240
in the right direction where
you are thinking as the LLM.

1190
00:53:54,240 --> 00:53:56,070
And the CPU, you have
the context window,

1191
00:53:56,070 --> 00:53:57,660
which is acting like a RAM.

1192
00:53:57,660 --> 00:54:00,240
And then you are trying
to build other utilities,

1193
00:54:00,240 --> 00:54:02,940
so you have an ethernet,
which is the browser.

1194
00:54:02,940 --> 00:54:05,130
You can have other LLMs
that you can talk to,

1195
00:54:05,130 --> 00:54:07,260
your file system
plus embeddings.

1196
00:54:07,260 --> 00:54:08,910
That's this part.

1197
00:54:08,910 --> 00:54:11,745
You have the software
1.0 classical tools

1198
00:54:11,745 --> 00:54:13,260
which the LLM control.

1199
00:54:13,260 --> 00:54:16,380
And then you can
add multimodality.

1200
00:54:16,380 --> 00:54:18,463
So this is more like,
you have video inputs,

1201
00:54:18,463 --> 00:54:19,380
you have audio inputs.

1202
00:54:19,380 --> 00:54:23,330
You have more things over time.

1203
00:54:23,330 --> 00:54:24,870
And then once you
look at this, you

1204
00:54:24,870 --> 00:54:27,780
start to see the whole picture
of where like things will go.

1205
00:54:27,780 --> 00:54:30,753
So currently, what we are
seeing mostly is just the LLM.

1206
00:54:30,753 --> 00:54:33,420
Most people are just working on
optimizing, making it very good,

1207
00:54:33,420 --> 00:54:34,990
but this is the
whole picture of what

1208
00:54:34,990 --> 00:54:38,680
we want to achieve for it
to be a useful system that

1209
00:54:38,680 --> 00:54:40,540
can actually do things for me.

1210
00:54:40,540 --> 00:54:44,012

1211
00:54:44,012 --> 00:54:45,470
And I think what
we'll start to see

1212
00:54:45,470 --> 00:54:48,320
is this becomes an
operating system instance,

1213
00:54:48,320 --> 00:54:51,620
where someone say, OpenAI can
go and build this whole thing,

1214
00:54:51,620 --> 00:54:53,480
and then I can plug in programs.

1215
00:54:53,480 --> 00:54:55,955
I can build stuff on top
of this operating system.

1216
00:54:55,955 --> 00:55:00,080

1217
00:55:00,080 --> 00:55:02,870
Here's also a even more
generalized concept

1218
00:55:02,870 --> 00:55:05,360
which I like to call
a neural computer.

1219
00:55:05,360 --> 00:55:09,860
And it's very similar,
but it's like, OK,

1220
00:55:09,860 --> 00:55:13,730
now if you were to think of this
as a fully-fledged computer,

1221
00:55:13,730 --> 00:55:16,555
what are the different
systems you need to do?

1222
00:55:16,555 --> 00:55:19,460
And you can think
of, maybe I'm a user.

1223
00:55:19,460 --> 00:55:21,910
I'm talking to this
sort of like a AI,

1224
00:55:21,910 --> 00:55:23,570
which is like a full-fledge AI.

1225
00:55:23,570 --> 00:55:26,087
Imagine the goal
is to build, what

1226
00:55:26,087 --> 00:55:27,920
should the architecture
of Jarvis look like?

1227
00:55:27,920 --> 00:55:30,920
And I'll say this goes
into the right architecture

1228
00:55:30,920 --> 00:55:33,440
to some extent, where you
can think, this is a user,

1229
00:55:33,440 --> 00:55:36,110
was talking to a Jarvis-like AI.

1230
00:55:36,110 --> 00:55:37,460
You have a chat interface.

1231
00:55:37,460 --> 00:55:40,850
The chat is how I'm
interacting with it,

1232
00:55:40,850 --> 00:55:42,860
which could be responsible
for personalization.

1233
00:55:42,860 --> 00:55:46,350
It can have some sort of
history about what I don't like.

1234
00:55:46,350 --> 00:55:49,710
So it has some layers which
are storing my preferences.

1235
00:55:49,710 --> 00:55:51,330
It knows how to communicate.

1236
00:55:51,330 --> 00:55:56,490
It has human-like,
maybe, empathy skills,

1237
00:55:56,490 --> 00:55:58,620
so it feels very human-like.

1238
00:55:58,620 --> 00:56:01,140
And after the
interface, you have

1239
00:56:01,140 --> 00:56:04,320
some sort of a task engine,
which is powering capabilities.

1240
00:56:04,320 --> 00:56:06,510
So if you ask it, OK, do
this calculation for me,

1241
00:56:06,510 --> 00:56:09,450
or find this, fetch
me this information,

1242
00:56:09,450 --> 00:56:14,490
or order me a burger, then
